# "What We Deliver" — Card Spec, five pillars
Drafted 2026-09-05 by docs-writer. **Spec only. `service-pillars.tsx` was read, never edited.**

## What this fixes, verified at source

The owner's complaint is literally true, and it is true for **three of the five pillars**. Verified by
grep against `src/components/home/service-pillars.tsx`:

| Pillar (`title:`) | Self-named card | Line |
|---|---|---|
| `Business Software.` (:133) | `title: "Business Software"` | **:158** |
| `Business Automation.` (:229) | `title: "Business Automation"` | **:254** |
| `AI Integration.` (:341) | `title: "AI Integration"` | **:369** |
| `Websites.` (:456) | none — first tile is `"Website Redesign"` | :486 |
| `Search, AI, and Ads.` (:576) | none — first tile is `"Local SEO"` | :599 |

Every card below is checked against Hard Rule 1. **No card carries its pillar's name.** The two
pillars that were already clean keep that property.

## Evidence ceiling — read this before trusting a row

I opened **one** implementation file: `src/components/home/service-pillars.tsx` (the website repo).
I did **not** open the nwks, marcommand or ps-admin repositories. Every `file:line` in the "ledger row"
column below is **quoted from the capability ledger**, which is itself a code-read artifact by another
agent. So a row here means *"the ledger cites this at that line"*, not *"I read that line."* Where the
ledger and the dispatch brief disagree, I report it under **Disagreements** rather than silently
picking one.

## Naming convention (read from the file)

Pillar titles are Title Case **with a trailing period** (`Websites.`, `AI Integration.`). Card titles
are Title Case **without** a period, and use `&` freely (`Client & Member Portals, Booking & Intake`).
Card titles below follow that.

**Pillar 5 rename:** `SEO AI Visibility Ad Management.` — the owner's five words unchanged, trailing
period added to match the four siblings. I did **not** insert commas or "and", because that would
change the words. If the implementer wants punctuation, that is a decision for the owner, not for me.

## The claim-tier rule as applied here

- **"We build X"** — free for BUILDABLE.
- **"We have built X"** — requires a SHIPPED or PARTIAL row.
- PARTIAL is never rounded to SHIPPED, and no card asserts a client story, a number, or a name that
  is not in the ledger.
- One extra guard the ledger imposes and I honoured: Lookout AI is PARTIAL, so *"we have built an
  assistant"* is available, but **any past-tense framing of it being in daily use is not**. It returns
  a 503 today (`routes/ai-ask.ts:215-227`).

