# PILLAR 1 — Business Software.

Over-supplied (12 SHIPPED). Selected for picturability, not for count. The self-named card at :158 is
deleted outright.

### Why the lead card leads
The owner's own framing of this pillar is *"custom stuff for them to get them off of spreadsheets, move
them into an entire system."* It is also the only card here backed by a **completed, one-time real
migration** rather than a feature — seven import scripts that ran end to end. Leading with the escape
puts the buyer's actual situation in card position 1, not a feature list.

---

**1. Off the Spreadsheet, Into One System**
*Body:* The workbook everyone has been emailing around becomes one system your whole team opens at
once — with history, permissions, and no `final_v3_REAL.xlsx`.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 1 — "Spreadsheet-to-D1 migration off the old Google Drive workflow",
**SHIPPED (one-time)** — `scripts/import_drive_documents.py`, `import_drive_export.py`,
`import_womens_drive.py`, `import_org_history.py`, `import_baptisms.py`,
`import_mbox_testimonies.py`, `build_slate_from_workbooks.py`. Reinforced by BUILDABLE P1 row 1.
*Claim check:* Body is present-tense capability = **"we build"**. **"We have built"** is separately
warranted here and may be used in adjacent prose — a real Google-Drive-to-database migration shipped.
No client is named, no number claimed.

**2. Exports Shaped for Whoever Asked for Them**
*Body:* Real Excel files in the shape the person receiving them needs — one to share, one to work
from, one grouped by location, one that feeds a label sheet — previewed on screen before it downloads.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 1 — "Roster export to real .xlsx with **four** named profiles",
**SHIPPED** — `admin/src/export/rosterWorkbook.ts:77`
(`export type ExportProfile = 'share' | 'working' | 'launch' | 'avery'`), writer at
`admin/src/export/xlsx.ts`, preview at `ExportPreviewTile.tsx`.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED. **See Disagreement #1: the brief says two profiles, the
ledger cites four.** The body names four shapes without printing the digit, so it survives either way.

**3. Badges, Tags & Labels Off the Live List**
*Body:* Name badges, tags and labels print straight from the list the event is actually running on,
onto standard label stock — no merge step, no stale CSV.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 1 — "Badge & tag printing, incl. Avery label layout", **SHIPPED** —
`admin/src/pages/PrintPage.tsx`, `admin/src/pages/print/`, plus the `'avery'` export profile.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED.

**4. One Board for Who's Assigned Where**
*Body:* Drag people onto rooms, crews or shifts and the board *is* the record — nobody is holding a
different version of it.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 1 — "Cabin / room assignment board", **SHIPPED** —
`admin/src/App.tsx:452` `<Route path="/rooms" .../>`, `admin/src/pages/rooms/`.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** for the room/cabin board (SHIPPED). The generalisation to crews and
shifts is **"we build"**. The body is written generically on purpose so it never asserts a shipped
crew-scheduling product that does not exist.

**5. A Staffing Schedule That Prints Itself**
*Body:* The staffing sheet your team argues over on Thursday comes out as a printed PDF schedule on
Friday, generated from the same records.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 1 — "Org Sheet staffing with schedule PDF", **SHIPPED** —
`admin/src/App.tsx:453`, `admin/src/pages/orgsheet/schedulePdf.ts`.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED.

**6. Their Own Login — Revoked the Day They Leave**
*Body:* Every person gets their own account with only the screens their role needs, a second factor
that is on unless someone deliberately turns it off, and access you take back in one click.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 1 — "Two-factor auth with a fail-secure off switch", **SHIPPED** —
`wrangler.toml:28-31` (only the exact string `"false"` disables it), parsed in
`functions/_api/security.ts twoFactorEnabled()`. Plus BUILDABLE P1 row 4 —
`functions/_api/auth.ts reviewerMayReach` as an **allowlist** (`routes/ai-ask.ts:9-13`),
`TeamPage.tsx`, `SecurityPage.tsx`.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED. The fail-secure detail (a typo turns 2FA back **on**) is
the differentiating half and it is quoted from the config, not invented.

**7. Contact Details That Delete Themselves on Schedule**
*Body:* Personal data comes out of the system when its retention window closes — a phone number that
disappears on the date you set, with a log of who looked at it before then.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 1 — "Lead Desk — lightweight multi-tenant CRM with sealed PII, access log,
retention sweep, response-time report", **SHIPPED (deployed worker)** — `src/leaddesk/` (31 files,
8,154 LOC), routed first at `src/worker.ts:50`, retention on the daily cron at `src/worker.ts:96`.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED. Deliberately placed: data security / privacy is the
**#1 adoption barrier at 33%, up from 23% in 2025** (Bluevine/Centiment, fielded 7–9 Apr 2026, n=942,
±3%). This card answers it with a shipped mechanism instead of a reassurance.

