# PILLAR 2 — Business Automation.

Owner's seed: manual data entry as the headline win, plus API integration and systems talking to each
other. **"MCP" appears nowhere below** — every business-audience article on it is still a definitional
explainer and there is zero credible SMB adoption data. It belongs in a technical FAQ. The self-named
card at :254 is deleted.

**No number appears on any card in this pillar.** The circulating "340% ROI", "2.3-month payback" and
"82% copy-paste between systems" figures trace only to content farms and are rejected.

### Why the lead card leads
The owner named manual data entry as the headline win, and it is the one complaint every buyer in this
category recognises in their own voice. It also sidesteps table-stakes claim #6 ("we integrate with
your existing tools") by leading with the *symptom* rather than the *integration*.

---

**1. Entered Once, Never Retyped**
*Body:* Your systems tell each other instead of someone retyping between them — fourteen outside
platforms already sweep into a single table on a schedule, and the same wiring points at the tools you
actually run.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 2 — "Scheduled connector sweep pulling 14 platforms into one fact table",
**SHIPPED** — `src/worker.ts:74` `handleConnectorScheduled(...)`, crons `["17 4 * * *", "23 */6 * * *"]`
at `wrangler.jsonc:112`. Supported by "Inbound-email → structured record pipeline", **SHIPPED**.
*Claim check:* Split, and the split is in the sentence. **"We have built"** the fourteen-platform sweep
(SHIPPED). Pointing it at a given client's tools is **"we build"** — the body says "the same wiring
points at", present tense capability, not a delivery record.

**2. It Arrives as an Email and Files Itself**
*Body:* Someone emails it in, the Word attachment gets opened and read, and the contents land on the
right record — the reviewer sees the actual story, not "Here's my testimony!".
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 2 — "Inbound-email → structured record pipeline with .docx text
extraction, dependency-free", **SHIPPED** — `email-worker/docx.ts` (432 lines, ZIP central-directory
walk + `DecompressionStream('deflate-raw')`, `MAX_DOCX_BYTES = 10_000_000` at `:46`), wired at
`email-worker/worker.ts:7-15`, rationale at `docx.ts:5-8`.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED. Honest limit worth keeping in the body if space allows:
`.doc` and `.pdf` are refused **on purpose** (`docx.ts:17-20`) because a naive byte-strip "produces
convincing garbage, which is worse than nothing."

**3. Approve It by Replying "Yes" From Your Phone**
*Body:* Reply to the email the way you would to a person — a plain "approved" goes straight through,
and anything carrying a change is held for you instead of guessed at.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 2 — "Email-reply-driven invoice approval", **SHIPPED** —
`agent/reply_ai.py:167-169` (deterministic regex fast path, no model call for a bare approval),
`agent/approval_watcher.py`, `agent/graph_mail.py`.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED, and running for invoices today. The body does not claim
it works on arbitrary document types; the ledger says pointing it at another type is the remaining work.

**4. Two Lists Reconciled Before You Get In**
*Body:* Payments against invoices, orders against shipments — the obvious matches are already done
when you open it, and you look only at the handful that are genuinely unclear.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 2 — "Bank-deposit ↔ invoice reconciliation with deterministic re-check",
**SHIPPED** — `agent/reconcile.py`, verdict re-validation at `agent/adjudicate.py:44-52` (exact amount,
open + unused invoice, confidence floor).
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED for payments/invoices. Orders-against-shipments is the
same mechanism pointed elsewhere; the body's list is a description of the *pattern*, so it reads as
"we build" for the second example. If that feels thin to the implementer, cut "orders against
shipments" — do not add a client.

**5. The Things It Will Never Do Without You**
*Body:* Every automation ships with a written list of what it is not allowed to do unless a person
says yes — and until you deliberately turn it loose, automated mail goes to you, with its real
recipient in the subject line.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 2 — "Unattended-email blast guard (fail-safe redirect to owner)",
**SHIPPED** — `wrangler.toml:38-52`; only the exact string `"live"` opens the gate. Reinforced by
`src/executor/kernel.ts:22-25` ("SHADOW IS THE DEFAULT AND IT IS NOT A NO-OP"), **SHIPPED**.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED, two independent implementations. Deliberately placed:
78% of SMB owners do not fully trust AI unsupervised and 31% name accuracy distrust as a barrier
(Bluevine, Apr 2026). This converts the buyer's fear into the specification. **Do not put the 2026-08-20
incident that mailed 13 admins on the card** — it is in the ledger and it is why the guard exists, but
it is a Preisser incident and the card is not the place to narrate it.

**6. When One Overnight Job Breaks, the Rest Still Run**
*Body:* A failure at 2am is recorded by name and the unrelated jobs still finish — you find out from
the log, not from a customer a week later.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 2 — "Daily retention sweeps that run even when a sibling job throws",
**SHIPPED** — `src/worker.ts:94-98` `Promise.allSettled([...])` with the reasoning at `:88-93`.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED.

