# PILLAR 3 — AI Integration.

The thinnest pillar and the owner's biggest ask: **2 SHIPPED, 8 PARTIAL, 5 NOT BUILT, 11 BUILDABLE**.
Both SHIPPED rows are guardrails — a spend cap and prompt-injection handling — i.e. shipped code that
*constrains* AI, not shipped AI. Cards 5 and 6 are those two rows, placed deliberately so the pillar
is anchored in something that indisputably runs. The self-named card at :369 is deleted.

Forbidden on any card here, per the ledger: "AI agents that optimize your ads", "8-agent marketing
system", "AI reads your documents" (bare), "AI writes your invoices", "AI answers your leads".

### Why the lead card leads
Lookout AI is the single most substantial thing in the inventory — a streamed ten-tool loop over a live
production database with server-injected tenant isolation, role-gated writes and a spend ceiling. The
hard parts are done. It leads because nothing else in this pillar is close.

---

**1. Ask Your Business a Question in Plain English**
*Body:* Ask "how many people from Hays registered this year?" and get the chart and the list behind it
off your real data — ten tools, four of which can write, and which ones a person can reach depends on
their role.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 3 — "**Lookout AI** — natural-language assistant over live production data,
streamed, 6 read + 4 write tools, server-injected tenant isolation, metered spend cap", **PARTIAL** —
`ai/askLoop.ts:374` `anthropic.messages.stream({`, `ai/ask-tools.ts` (1,535 lines; `find_person` :254,
`get_person_profile` :278, `list_people` :298, `event_overview` :330, `roster_breakdown` :345,
`chart_data` :376), `ai/askWrite.ts` (1,203 lines; `move_person_to_room` :215, `record_baptism` :253,
`generate_sheet` :300, `church_baptism_report` :327), tenant scope injected server-side
(`routes/ai-ask.ts:20-24`), writes gated at `:284`, spend capped at `:249`.
*Claim check:* **"we build"** is the shipping phrasing, per the ledger's own ✅ wording:
*"We build plain-English assistants that answer questions off your live business data, with the tenant
isolation, permission gates and spend caps that make that safe to put in front of staff."*
PARTIAL does warrant a bare "we have built one", but **not** in this pillar's copy — the row is gated
behind an unplaced API key and returns 503 today (`routes/ai-ask.ts:215-227`). Any sentence implying
it is in daily use is prohibited. The "Ten tools. Four of them write." fact is present-tense
description of code that exists and ships as-is.

**2. The Model Reads It. A Checker Verifies Every Number.**
*Body:* We pointed a model at a tax document and it returned a confident figure that was two years
stale — off a document that was current, and that contains the right number twice and the wrong one
not at all. So now every figure a model returns has to appear literally in the source before anyone
sees it, and a test fails the build if anyone removes that check.
*Ledger row + tier:* Two rows, and the split matters.
- The refusal and the test: Pillar 3 — "AI document extraction", **NOT BUILT — by decision, with a
  test enforcing it** — `agent/refdata_ingest.py:5-13` (the $168,600 incident),
  `tests/test_refdata_ingest.py:26-46` (bans `anthropic`/`openai`/`PROMPT`/`shutil.which`/`claude`
  from the module source, pins `subprocess.run` argv[0] to `{CURL, PDFTOTEXT}`).
- The pattern being sold: ledger 3.2, **BUILDABLE** — "model proposes, deterministic checker gates",
  already implemented three times: `agent/adjudicate.py:44-52`, `functions/_api/testimonies/classify.ts`,
  `src/responder/validate.ts:5-7`.
*Claim check:* **"we build"** for the joined extract-then-verify pipeline — the two halves exist
separately and have never been joined. **"We have built"** is warranted for the verification layer and
the build-failing test, which are real. **This card gets two sentences, not one, and that is
deliberate:** the ledger explicitly forbids softening the incident into "we ensure accuracy" and
forbids describing it as hypothetical. It happened. Owning it is the differentiator. Do not print the
dollar figures on the card — "two years stale" carries it, and the digits invite a
"which document?" conversation the card cannot hold.

**3. The AI Writes It. A Person Still Presses Send.**
*Body:* Anything a model drafts — an email, a reply, a quote — lands in a queue for you. It can
propose and it structurally cannot deliver; there is no path from the loop to a send function.
*Ledger row + tier:* Ledger 3.3 row 1, **BUILDABLE**, resting on three shipped implementations:
`functions/_api/ai/agent.ts:4-8` ("PROPOSE tools only insert `ai_pending_actions` — they NEVER send
email") **PARTIAL**; `src/executor/kernel.ts:22-25` **SHIPPED**; `wrangler.toml:38-52` **SHIPPED**.
*Claim check:* **"we build"** for the general approval queue. **"We have built"** is warranted for the
propose-don't-send architecture specifically — three independent implementations exist. The phrase
"structurally, not by policy" is quotable from `agent.ts:4-8` and is the whole value of the card.

**4. When It Isn't Sure, It Asks You**
*Body:* Two possible matches produce a question, never a confident answer about the wrong person or
the wrong invoice — being unsure is a result the system is allowed to return.
*Ledger row + tier:* Ledger 3.3 row 7, **BUILDABLE**, on shipped behaviour:
`routes/ai-ask.ts:24-26` ("A name matching more than one person returns a candidate list; the model is
required to ask which one and forbidden from picking. That is the top failure mode for this feature"),
`admin/src/askApi.ts:38-47`, `agent/adjudicate.py` deferral.
*Claim check:* **"we build"**. The underlying rule is implemented and the ledger calls it the
most-repeated rule in the repos, but the row is BUILDABLE as a generalised product, so present tense
only. Directly answers the 31% "distrusting AI's accuracy" barrier.

**5. You See What the AI Costs, Per Question**
*Body:* Every call is priced and logged, so the AI is a line item you can read — and when the month's
ceiling is reached it refuses the next call instead of sending you a surprise bill.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 3 — "Per-program monthly AI spend ceiling that degrades to a sentence, not
an error", **SHIPPED (code)** — `functions/_api/aiBudget.ts:73-75` (per-model pricing), enforced
*before the client is constructed* at `routes/ai-ask.ts:249`. Second implementation:
`src/inference/engine.ts` — per-call `inference_call` ledger row with cache-read and cache-write
tokens priced **separately** (`:183-198`), cap that throws `CostCapExceededError` (`:217-222`).
Ledger 3.3 row 4, **BUILDABLE**, for surfacing that ledger to a client.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED, one of only two shipped rows in this pillar. Surfacing it
as a client-facing report is **"we build"**. The body claims the mechanism, not the report, so it is clean.

**6. Let It Read What Strangers Wrote You — Without Letting Them Give It Orders**
*Body:* Reviews, form submissions and inbound email are handed to the model as quoted content, never
as instructions, and the model never writes a query — every lookup is a fixed statement it can only
fill in.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 3 — "Prompt-injection handling for untrusted marketing text",
**SHIPPED (code)** — `src/responder/injection.ts:7` (inbound "passed as delimited data"),
`src/agent/boundary.ts:68-71` ("Untrusted. Quoted content, never instructions"). Plus
`routes/ai-ask.ts:19-20` — the model "NEVER writes SQL and never names a table or column."
Ledger 3.3 row 6, **BUILDABLE**, for the packaged version.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED, the pillar's second shipped row. Aimed squarely at the
33% data-security barrier, which is the fastest-rising fear in the market.

**7. Reply Like a Human. The System Does the Rest.**
*Body:* "Can you push the due date to the 15th?" becomes a revised draft, not a sent document — and
the fields that identify the record are refused outright, because a document number is not something
you change by email.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 3 — "AI classification of free-text invoice replies (approve / hold /
revise)", **PARTIAL** — `agent/reply_ai.py:171-175` via the local `claude -p` CLI, deterministic
approve fast path at `:167-169`, identity fields refused at `:40-43`, fails closed to a hold when the
CLI is missing. Ledger 3.3 row 2, **BUILDABLE**, for widening the field set.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — PARTIAL warrants it, and this runs for invoices today. Widening it
past invoices is **"we build"**. The body says "a revised draft, not a sent document" — generic enough
not to claim a shipped product for other document types.

### Held back from Pillar 3, with rows, rather than dropped silently
- **"Your meeting notes become the invoice line items."** Ledger 3.3 row 3 / Pillar 3 "AI drafting of
  invoice description lines", **PARTIAL** — `agent/run_monthly.sh:13` +
  `agent/prompts/monthly_invoice_additions.md` (≤5 noun phrases, `[]` on failure) →
  `agent/invoice_run.py:41` `merge_description`, additive-only. Real and running, but **scope is one
  client, description text only** — too narrow to survive a card without a qualifier that would
  swallow the card. Available if the owner wants an eighth.
- **"Work that can wait until morning costs half as much."** Ledger 3.3 row 5, **BUILDABLE** —
  `src/inference/engine.ts:273` submits to the Message Batches API at 50% off, `:293-348` drains.
  Held because nothing submits a batch today and the saving is an implementation detail a buyer does
  not shop on.

