# PILLAR 4 — Websites.

This pillar already has no self-named card (:486 is `"Website Redesign"`), so Hard Rule 1 was not the
problem here — vagueness was. Every card below names a thing the buyer can picture.

### Why the lead card leads
The two loudest fears in the website-buying literature are **ownership** and **"can I edit it myself,
and what exactly can I change?"** — with the observation that *"'Yes, with training' and 'yes, anything'
are different answers."* We have a shipped in-browser editor, so we can answer the harder version of
that question in card position 1. Table-stakes claims ("mobile-responsive", "fast", "SEO-friendly")
are barred from the lead slot; a buyer would be shocked by their absence, not impressed by their
presence.

---

**1. Change Your Own Words Without Calling Us**
*Body:* Edit the text on your own site from an admin screen — no developer, no deploy, no invoice for
a sentence.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 4 — "In-browser web page editor (client edits their own copy)",
**SHIPPED** — `admin/src/App.tsx:507` `<Route path="/page-details" element={<PageDetails />} />`,
`admin/src/pages/PageDetails.tsx`, `page-editor.css`. BUILDABLE P4 row 1 adds
`scripts/gen-page-document-seed.mjs` and `scripts/check-page-document.mjs`.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED.

**2. A Form That Goes Into the System, Not an Inbox**
*Body:* Forms write straight into the database the rest of your business runs on, assigned to somebody
— instead of an email notification a person has to re-key.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 4 — "Form builder with a seeded field catalogue", **SHIPPED** —
`admin/src/App.tsx:506` `<Route path="/forms" element={<FormsEditor />} />`,
`scripts/gen-form-fields-seed.mjs`, over the same D1 database the admin reads.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED.

**3. One Address for the Site and the Software Behind It**
*Body:* The public site and the application your team logs into are served from one place — one
domain, one login, and a path that does not exist returns a real 404 instead of a page pretending
otherwise.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 4 — "Hash-routed SPA served as Worker static assets alongside a JSON API
on one origin", **SHIPPED** — `marcommand-main/wrangler.jsonc` `"assets": {"directory": "./dist"}`
with the reasoning at ~lines 245-261. BUILDABLE P4 row 3.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED. This is the honest version of the owner's *"sites wired
into their systems."*

**4. Documents That Print Exactly Right, Every Time**
*Body:* Invoices, statements and certificates render to a fixed page, so the footer does not slide
onto page two when the description runs long.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 4 — "Public-facing invoice/document rendering to print-exact PDF",
**SHIPPED** — `renderer/render.py` + `renderer/template.html` (letter-size canvas, headless Chrome),
already producing monthly invoices.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED.

**5. Your Links Keep Working When the Domain Moves**
*Body:* Every email link and embedded image is built from one configured set of addresses, so changing
your domain does not blank the logo in somebody's inbox.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 4 — "Public marketing site + admin on custom domains, Cloudflare-hosted",
**SHIPPED** — `functions/_api/origins.ts resolveOrigins()` as the single reader of
`PUBLIC_ORIGIN` / `ADMIN_ORIGIN` / `ASSET_ORIGIN` (`wrangler.toml:56-60`), pinned by `origins.test.ts`.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED.

**6. Publish a Page, Tell the Search Engines the Same Minute**
*Body:* New and changed pages are submitted for indexing the moment they go up, instead of waiting to
be crawled.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 5 — "IndexNow submission — the one real platform write in the product",
**SHIPPED** — `src/connectors/indexnow/adapter.ts` (359 lines),
`DECISIONS/0002-indexnow-submission-is-a-platform-write.md`. BUILDABLE P5 row 2: firing it from a
content change is wiring.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** the submission adapter (SHIPPED); **"we build"** the publish-time
trigger. Body says "are submitted", which describes the deliverable without claiming an existing
integration. Cross-pillar borrow, declared: the row lives under Pillar 5 in the ledger but the
deliverable is a website build item and the owner listed AI/search readiness under Websites.

### The Google tension, and how this pillar handles it
The owner's raw list includes *"maximum SEO, AEO and GEO."* Google Search Central, updated 2026-07-10,
says verbatim: *"From Google Search's perspective, optimizing for generative AI search is optimizing
for the search experience, and thus still SEO,"* mythbusts `llms.txt` and special schema, and tells
buyers to *"review our guidance on evaluating third-party SEO advice"* before hiring an AEO/GEO vendor.
Stacking three acronyms as three products on the Websites cards invites exactly that scrutiny, and no
consensus definition distinguishes GEO from AEO from AIO anyway.

**Decision:** Websites carries no acronym card. The owner's meaning — *build it so search and AI
answers can both use it* — is delivered by cards 3 and 6, which are concrete and shipped. The
acronym question is answered head-on in Pillar 5 card 7, where quoting Google *against* our own
category is a credibility move rather than a dodge.

### Owner list items I could not source, stated plainly
- **E-commerce.** No row in any pillar. The nearest thing is `agent/stripe_pay.py` (Pillar 1 SHIPPED
  "Books / ledger, reconciliation, Plaid link, Stripe pay") which is payment collection, not a
  storefront. **No card written.**
- **"Psychology lead funnel."** No row anywhere. **No card written.**
- **"Granular tracking down to where a visitor dropped off."** Sourced, but the row is Pillar 5's
  first-party analytics stack — it is delivered as Pillar 5 card 5 rather than duplicated here.
- **"Custom web applications."** Sourced (Pillar 1 "Multi-screen admin SPA over a live D1 database",
  SHIPPED), but as a Websites card it would restate Pillar 1 and read like a category, not a
  deliverable. Deliberately not written.

---

# PILLAR 5 — SEO AI Visibility Ad Management.

Renamed from `Search, AI, and Ads.` (:576). The owner: *"we really need to come up with those things
that are going to set us apart on this one."* This pillar has 8 SHIPPED rows and 2 NOT BUILT, and the
NOT BUILT one is the AI-visibility measurement everybody wants. That shapes the claim phrasing below
more than anything else on the page.

**Barred from this pillar:** the "84% of AI citations come from earned media" figure (flagged
UNVERIFIED; the research said drop it rather than soften it) and the "38% of AI-cited sources rank in
Google's top 10" figure (UNVERIFIED and in direct tension with Google's own position).

### Why the lead card leads
"Cited vs. absorbed" is the best-sourced differentiator available anywhere in this project: arXiv
2604.25707, 30 April 2026, 602 prompts and 21,143 valid citations analysed, establishing that whether
an AI *retrieves* your page and whether your content *makes it into the answer* are driven by
different factors. It is [A]-sourced, four months old, and no SMB competitor mentions it. It is also
the one framing that survives Google's mythbusting, because it measures rather than hacks.

---

**1. Cited, or Actually Used — We Report Both**
*Body:* Being linked under an AI answer and being the thing the answer is made of are two different
outcomes, and only one of them wins the customer — so we report them separately.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 5 — "AI-search / AEO visibility measurement", **NOT BUILT** → BUILDABLE
P5 row 1. The adjacency argument is real: the connector interface is merged with **fourteen** adapters
written against it (`src/connectors/`, ~20,500 LOC), plus `scheduler.ts`, `syncHealth.ts` and a shared
fact table — a new source plugs into finished machinery. External evidence: arXiv 2604.25707 [A].
*Claim check:* **"we build" ONLY.** The ledger's instruction is explicit — *"Say 'we build this', never
'we measure this today' — no code for it exists"* (searched all 14 connector folders, all 15
migrations, `src/brief/signals.ts`). The body is written in the deliverable-future voice: "we report
them separately" describes what the service produces, and must not be accompanied anywhere on the page
by a claim that this is running for anyone today. **This is the pillar's honest gap and the card most
likely to be laundered by a later editor. Flag it in the code comment.**

**2. The Real Questions Your Buyers Ask an Assistant**
*Body:* We build the list of actual buying questions in your market, run them across the assistants
your customers use, and show you every answer you are missing from — alongside whoever is in there
instead.
*Ledger row + tier:* Same row — Pillar 5 "AI-search / AEO visibility measurement", **NOT BUILT** →
BUILDABLE P5 row 1. External framing: the tooling market's actual definition, stripped of jargon.
*Claim check:* **"we build" ONLY**, same constraint as card 1. Note the body says *"the list of actual
buying questions"* and not a count — the research's illustrative "~50" is not a commitment anyone has
made, so it does not go on the card. **Declared concentration:** cards 1 and 2 both rest on a row with
zero code today. That is two of seven. It is defensible because the adjacency argument is strong and
both are phrased as "we build", but the implementer should not add a third.

**3. All Your Ad Spend in One Table — With the Mismatches Flagged**
*Body:* Google, Meta, Microsoft, LinkedIn, TikTok, Reddit, Pinterest and Nextdoor spend in one place,
each row stamped with how it was measured, so nobody adds two numbers that mean different things.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 5 — "Ad spend from 8 paid platforms, normalised, with attribution windows
stamped per row", **SHIPPED** — `src/connectors/meta/adapter.ts:33-41` records that Meta's default is
7-day click **plus** 1-day view while Google attributes to the click date and counts no view-throughs,
and stamps `native_attribution_window` on every row. BUILDABLE P5 row 3 for the client-facing report.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED, and the eight platform names are read from the ledger,
not assembled. The client-facing *report* over that table is **"we build"**; the body describes the
table, so it is clean. The refusal to fudge is the differentiator — it directly answers *"every
platform reports a different number and I don't know which to believe."*

**4. Every Morning, the Five Things Costing You Money**
*Body:* A ranked daily list — money going out with nothing coming back, leads nobody answered, a
channel that quietly stopped reporting — and an explicit note about anything it could not see.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 5 — "Daily ranked brief", **SHIPPED (deterministic)** —
`src/brief/brief.ts:36-39`, six signals: `untouchedLeads`, `slowResponse`, `brokenConnections`,
`failingConnections`, `nothingConnected`, `spendWithoutConversions`. Blind-spot reporting at
`brief.ts:15-20`, **SHIPPED**.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED. **Do not attach "AI" to this card in any form.** The
ledger is emphatic: "**No model involved**." It is six SQL signals ranked. Saying otherwise would be
the exact failure this spec exists to prevent — and the honesty ("an explicit note about anything it
could not see") is worth more than the AI label.

**5. Your Own Analytics, Not Somebody Else's**
*Body:* Your own script on your own domain, your own sessions, your own retention window — you can see
where a visitor dropped off without renting the answer back from a third party.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 5 — "First-party site analytics: own snippet, own sessionizer, own funnel",
**SHIPPED** — `src/snippet/` (size-budgeted by `scripts/check-snippet-budget.mjs`), `src/sessionizer/`
(Durable Object), `src/collector/` (queue + DO rate limiter), retention on the daily cron
(`src/worker.ts:96`). BUILDABLE P5 row 5: installing it on a client site is deployment.
*Claim check:* **"we have built"** — SHIPPED. Delivers the owner's Websites item *"granular tracking
down to where a visitor dropped off"*, and doubles as an ownership/data-security answer (the 33%
barrier) without making a security promise the code does not back.

**6. AI Traffic Counted as Its Own Channel**
*Body:* Visitors who arrive from an AI assistant are tracked as their own channel with their own
conversion rate, instead of being buried in "referral" or "direct" where you cannot see them.
*Ledger row + tier:* Pillar 5 — "First-party site analytics", **SHIPPED** (same stack as card 5: own
snippet, own sessionizer, so the referrer split is ours to define). The AI-referrer segmentation
itself is **BUILDABLE** — no ledger row names it. External evidence: Adobe Digital Insights Q1 2026
[A], >1 trillion US retail visits — AI-referred traffic converted 38% *worse* than non-AI in March
2025 and 42% *better* in March 2026.
*Claim check:* **"we build"** — the segmentation does not exist as code. The body is present-tense
deliverable voice with no delivery record claimed. **The Adobe numbers do not go on the card.** The
panel is US retail, not SMB services; the research is explicit that it must never be presented as
"your business will see this." If the owner wants the number in body copy elsewhere, the only safe
framing is *"the direction of the market flipped inside twelve months, measured across a trillion
visits."* And the vendor "4.4x–23x" multiples are rejected outright.

**7. Google's Own Words on AEO and GEO**
*Body:* Google's July 2026 guidance says optimizing for AI search is still SEO, and that you do not
need `llms.txt`, special schema, or content chopped into pieces. We agree. Here is the short list of
what genuinely is different — and what we will do about it.
*Ledger row + tier:* **No ledger row, and it does not need one — this card makes no delivery claim.**
It is a positioning statement sourced to Google Search Central,
`developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide`, last updated **2026-07-10**
[A]. Verbatim: *"From Google Search's perspective, optimizing for generative AI search is optimizing
for the search experience, and thus still SEO."* Also verbatim: *"You don't need to create new machine
readable files, AI text files, markup, or Markdown to appear in Google Search"* and *"Structured data
isn't required for generative AI search, and there's no special schema.org markup you need to add."*
*Claim check:* Neither "we build" nor "we have built" — it is a stated position, and the only
first-person commitment is *"here is the short list… and what we will do about it,"* which is a promise
of a document, not of a capability. **That promise creates an obligation: the short list has to
actually exist somewhere on the site before this ships.** Flag to the implementer. Every competitor
sells AEO and GEO as two premium products while Google publicly warns buyers about exactly that;
quoting Google against our own category is a credibility move no competitor will copy.

### Held back from Pillar 5, marked rather than dropped
> **Unverified:** *"Performance Max channel-level and search-term reporting, monthly"* — *"You will see
> which channels your Performance Max budget actually went to and which search terms it actually
> matched."* This is a genuine 2026-specific edge with a 12–18 month shelf life before it commoditises,
> and it directly answers the loudest agency complaint (*"monthly reports with metrics they can't tie
> to revenue"*). **Not confirmed against the implementation, and not confirmed against Google.** The
> feature list (channel-level reporting, full search-term reporting, campaign-level negative keywords
> up to 10,000, asset-level reporting) comes only from PPC blogs; the research explicitly did not check
> it against Google's own release notes, and there is no Preisser ledger row for reading or reporting
> PMax data — the Google Ads adapter is read-only spend ingestion with an empty action list
> (`src/connectors/googleAds/adapter.ts:63`). **What would confirm it:** Google Ads release notes for
> the 2026 PMax update, plus a decision on whether we report it from the Google Ads UI or from our own
> table. **DO NOT SHIP THIS CARD until both are done.** It is written here so it is not lost.

